#For GitHub to recognize your code owners, you must create a file named CODEOWNERS (no extension) in one of the following three locations in your repository:
#The root directory (most common)
#The .github/ directory (best for keeping the root clean)
#The docs/ directory

#The syntax for the CODEOWNERS file is straightforward. Each line consists of a file pattern followed by one or more GitHub usernames or team names that are responsible for those files.

#Define the Rules
#The syntax is simple: [Pattern] [Owner]. You can use GitHub usernames or team names (if the repo is in an Organization).
#
#   Pattern       Owner              Result
#   *             @global-owner      This person owns every file in the repo
#   *.js          @js-expert         Owns all JavaScript files
#   /scripts/     @devops-team       Owns everything in the root /scripts folder
#   docs/**/*     @writer            Owns all files inside the docs directory and its subdirectories
#
#
#Enable "Required Reviews" (Crucial Step)
#
#Simply adding the file doesn't stop people from merging code without the owner's permission. To make it "strict," you need to update your branch settings:
#Go to your repo on GitHub and click Settings
#On the left sidebar, click Branches
#Under Branch protection rules, click Edit on your main branch (or create a new rule)
#Check the box Require a pull request before merging
#Check the box Require review from Code Owners

#Important Tips:
#Permissions: Code owners must have write access to the repository to be automatically assigned as reviewers
#Case Sensitivity: File paths are case-sensitive on GitHub
#Teams: If you use a team (e.g., @org/team-alpha), ensure the team has explicit write access to the repo

